  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef likes sequences of integers and in particular, he likes arithmetic progressions.\n\nThere are two integers NNN and KKK. Chef wants to choose a starting positive integer AAA, create the sequence (A,A+K,A+2⋅K,…,A+(N−1)⋅K)(A, A+K, A + 2 \\cdot K, \\ldots, A + (N-1) \\cdot K)(A,A+K,A+2⋅K,…,A+(N−1)⋅K) and compute the product of all elements of this sequence; let's denote this product by XXX. For example, if N=3N=3N=3 and K=2K=2K=2, then Chef can create the sequence (4,6,8)(4,6,8)(4,6,8), for which X=192X=192X=192.\n\nFind the largest integer that divides XXX regardless of the value of AAA that Chef chooses. Since the answer can be large, compute it modulo 1,000,000,0071,000,000,0071,000,000,007 (109+710^9 + 7109+7).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤101 \\le T \\le 101≤T≤10",
      "1≤N≤1061 \\le N \\le 10^61≤N≤106",
      "1≤K≤10181 \\le K \\le 10^{18}1≤K≤1018"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and KKK.",
    "outputFormat": "For each test case, print a single line containing one integer — the largest number that always divides XXX, modulo 1,000,000,0071,000,000,0071,000,000,007.",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n2 2\n3 1",
        "output": "1\n6",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nExample case 1: If Chef chooses A=1A=1A=1, the sequence he creates is (1,3)(1,3)(1,3) and then, XXX is 333, and if he chooses A=2A=2A=2, the sequence is (2,4)(2,4)(2,4) and XXX is 888. We can see that the largest integer that divides them both is 111.\nExample case 2: The product of any 333 consecutive integers is always divisible by 666."
      }
    ]
  },
